  "why": "The statement is Beilinson's conjectures on special values of L-functions: the regulator is an isomorphism (after realification) and leading Taylor coefficients equal the regulator determinant up to a rational, for all smooth projective varieties over number fields. Nothing in the record proves this for any variety or integer point unconditionally beyond what was known. The numerical L-value/regulator checks for named curves and motives (de Jeu et al., Golyshev–Kerr, Nemoto–Yamauchi, Brunault–Lilienfeldt–Nemoto, Alfarano et al.) are finitely many numerical instances and are scored instances; their regulator non-vanishing results show certain classes are non-zero, which neither proves the rational-structure statement nor the L-value identity for any variety. Albanese-kernel / zero-cycle results (Banerjee, Gazaki–Love), Beilinson–Soulé (Déglise), Bloch–Kato and Beilinson–Bloch–Kato results (Grossi–Loeffler–Zerbes, Kim–Pollack, Peng, Dummigan et al.), the function-field analogues (Gazda, Broe) and Brunault–Chida's weak form are different statements sharing the name or the circle, so variant. Trieu assumes the conjecture (none); Brunault 2020 conjectures proportionality to Beilinson elements (none). On AI, Alfarano et al.'s quotes describe Claude processing, cleaning and finding patterns in data and Axolver training networks that do not predict r_k exactly; the paper does not attribute any proved or conjectured content in the result to either, so none.",
  "doubts": "Closest calls: (1) Golyshev–Kerr proving a Beilinson Hodge-type statement for specific Calabi–Yau families could be read as a case of 'Beilinson's conjectures' if the plural is read as the whole circle; I read the bullet's special-values wording. (2) Claude's 'identify patterns' role in 2608.00615 could be load-bearing for observed patterns, but the record says the paper does not identify which patterns, and the event's scope is instances regardless. (3) The frozen bullet wording was not confirmed from the January 2024 revision.",
